\section*{1. Derivation of the Two-Degree-of-Freedom Model}

We begin with the glucose control model introduced in lecture,
which consists of an integral equation and a differential equation:
\begin{equation}
u=\alpha v+\beta\int_{-\infty}^{t}\lambda e^{-\lambda(t-t')}v(t')\,dt',
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\dot v=-\gamma v-\delta u(v+\bar v)+F(t).
\end{equation}

Our goal is to eliminate the integral so that the model can be
expressed as a system of two ordinary differential equations.
To do this, we first differentiate the equation for \(u\)
with respect to time:
\begin{align}
\dot u&=\alpha\dot v+\beta\lambda\frac{d}{dt}
\left(\int_{-\infty}^{t}e^{-\lambda(t-t')}v(t')\,dt'\right).
\end{align}

Since the upper limit of integration depends on \(t\),
we apply Leibniz's rule:
\begin{align}
\dot u
&=\alpha\dot v+\beta\lambda\left(v(t)-\lambda
\int_{-\infty}^{t}e^{-\lambda(t-t')}v(t')\,dt'\right)\\
&=\alpha\dot v+\beta\lambda v-\lambda
\left(\beta\int_{-\infty}^{t}\lambda e^{-\lambda(t-t')}v(t')\,dt'\right).
\end{align}

Notice that the remaining integral is the same one that
appears in our original equation for \(u\). Rearranging
that equation gives:
\begin{equation}
\beta\int_{-\infty}^{t}\lambda e^{-\lambda(t-t')}v(t')\,dt'=u-\alpha v.
\end{equation}

We can now substitute this expression into our derivative:
\begin{align}
\dot u&=\alpha\dot v+\beta\lambda v-\lambda(u-\alpha v)\\
&=\alpha\dot v+\lambda(\alpha+\beta)v-\lambda u.
\end{align}

Finally, we introduce the notation:
\begin{equation}
\dot v=f(u,v,t)=-\gamma v-\delta u(v+\bar v)+F(t).
\end{equation}

This allows us to express the original model entirely in
terms of two differential equations:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\dot u&=-\lambda u+\lambda(\alpha+\beta)v+\alpha f(u,v,t),\\
\dot v&=-\gamma v-\delta u(v+\bar v)+F(t).
\end{aligned}}
\end{equation}

We have therefore obtained the required two-degree-of-freedom
ODE system without the integral term.

\section*{2. Scaling the Variables}

We now want to simplify the system by introducing dimensionless
variables, reducing the number of parameters appearing in
the equations.

Recall the system obtained in Question 1:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\dot u&=-\lambda u+\lambda(\alpha+\beta)v+\alpha f(u,v,t),\\
\dot v&=-\gamma v-\delta u(v+\bar v)+F(t).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

Following the scaling approach from lecture, we introduce:
\begin{equation}
t=\tilde t\,t',\qquad u=\tilde u\,u',\qquad v=\bar v\,v'.
\end{equation}

Because time is also being rescaled, we must account for
this when transforming the derivatives:
\begin{equation}
\dot u=\frac{\tilde u}{\tilde t}\frac{du'}{dt'},
\qquad
\dot v=\frac{\bar v}{\tilde t}\frac{dv'}{dt'}.
\end{equation}

We also define the dimensionless function \(f'\) by:
\begin{equation}
f'(u',v',t')=\frac{\tilde t}{\bar v}
f(\tilde u u',\bar v v',\tilde t t').
\end{equation}

Substituting the new variables into both equations gives:
\begin{align}
\frac{\tilde u}{\tilde t}\frac{du'}{dt'}
&=-\lambda\tilde u u'+\lambda(\alpha+\beta)\bar v v'
+\alpha\frac{\bar v}{\tilde t}f'(u',v',t'),\\
\frac{\bar v}{\tilde t}\frac{dv'}{dt'}
&=-\gamma\bar v v'-\delta\tilde u u'(\bar v v'+\bar v)+F(\tilde t t').
\end{align}

Next, we isolate the derivatives by dividing through
by their respective coefficients:
\begin{align}
\frac{du'}{dt'}
&=-(\lambda\tilde t)u'
+\frac{\lambda\bar v\tilde t}{\tilde u}(\alpha+\beta)v'
+\frac{\alpha\bar v}{\tilde u}f'(u',v',t'),\\
\frac{dv'}{dt'}
&=-(\gamma\tilde t)v'-(\delta\tilde u\tilde t)u'(v'+1)
+\frac{\tilde t}{\bar v}F(\tilde t t').
\end{align}

To make the second equation simpler, we choose the time
and \(u\)-scales such that:
\begin{equation}
\gamma\tilde t=1,\qquad \delta\tilde u\tilde t=1.
\end{equation}

This gives:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\tilde t=\frac1\gamma,\qquad
\tilde u=\frac{1}{\delta\tilde t}=\frac\gamma\delta.}
\end{equation}

We also introduce the scaled input:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{G(t')=\frac{F(\tilde t t')}{\gamma\bar v}.}
\end{equation}

With these choices, the system becomes:
\begin{align}
\frac{du'}{dt'}
&=-\frac\lambda\gamma u'
+\frac{\lambda\delta\bar v}{\gamma^2}(\alpha+\beta)v'
+\frac{\alpha\delta\bar v}{\gamma}f'(u',v',t'),\\
\frac{dv'}{dt'}&=-v'-u'(v'+1)+G(t').
\end{align}

We can simplify the notation further by defining
three new parameters:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{a=\frac\lambda\gamma,\qquad
b=\frac{\lambda\delta\bar v}{\gamma^2}(\alpha+\beta),\qquad
c=\frac{\alpha\delta\bar v}{\gamma}.}
\end{equation}

Using these definitions, the dimensionless system
can be written more compactly as:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\frac{du'}{dt'}&=-au'+bv'+cf'(u',v',t'),\\
\frac{dv'}{dt'}&=f'(u',v',t')=-v'-u'(v'+1)+G(t').
\end{aligned}}
\end{equation}

For the equilibrium calculations and numerical experiments,
we take \(G\) to be constant. This completes the scaling
process, leaving us with the reduced parameters \(a\),
\(b\), \(c\), and \(G\).

\section*{3. Equilibrium Solutions}

To determine the equilibrium solutions, we set both
time derivatives equal to zero. Since our equations
are now dimensionless, we can drop the primes
for simplicity.

The equilibrium conditions are:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
-au+bv&=0,\\
-v-u(v+1)+G&=0.
\end{aligned}}
\end{equation}

At equilibrium, \(f(u,v)=0\) because it represents
the \(v\)-equation, whose derivative must also vanish.

We begin by solving the first equation for \(u\):
\begin{equation}
-au+bv=0.
\end{equation}

Rearranging gives:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{u^*=\frac ba v^*.}
\end{equation}

We then substitute this relationship into the second
equilibrium equation:
\begin{equation}
-v-\frac ba v(v+1)+G=0.
\end{equation}

Expanding and collecting terms:
\begin{align}
-\frac ba v^2-\frac ba v-v+G&=0,\\
v^2+\left(1+\frac ab\right)v-\frac ab G&=0.
\end{align}

This is a quadratic equation in \(v\), so applying
the quadratic formula gives:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{v^*=-\frac12\left(1+\frac ab\right)
\pm\frac12\sqrt{\left(1+\frac ab\right)^2+\frac{4a}{b}G}.}
\end{equation}

The number of real equilibrium solutions depends on
the expression inside the square root. In particular,
the two solutions coincide when:
\begin{equation}
\left(1+\frac ab\right)^2+\frac{4a}{b}G=0.
\end{equation}

Solving this equation for \(G\) gives the critical
saddle-node value:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{G_{\mathrm{SN}}=-\frac b{4a}\left(1+\frac ab\right)^2.}
\end{equation}

Assuming \(a>0\) and \(b>0\), this critical value
separates the three possible cases. For
\(G<G_{\mathrm{SN}}\), there are no real equilibria.
At \(G=G_{\mathrm{SN}}\), the two solutions merge
into one, while for \(G>G_{\mathrm{SN}}\), two
distinct real equilibria exist.

We can summarize these results in the following table:
