\section*{4. Numerical Tests of Controllability}

To investigate the controllability of the non-dimensional system, numerical experiments were conducted using the supplied Python script \texttt{g\_simul.py}. Following Question 3, parameter values $a = 0.1$ and $b = 1.0$ were held fixed, giving a saddle-node threshold of:
\begin{equation}
G_{\mathrm{SN}} = -\frac{1}{4(0.1)}(1.1)^2 = -3.025.
\end{equation}

Trajectories were simulated using a second-order Runge-Kutta scheme across representative values of $G$ selected from each of the three parameter regimes identified in Question 3:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Case 1 ($G < G_{\mathrm{SN}}$):} $G = -4.0$ (no equilibrium points).
    \item \textbf{Case 2 ($G = G_{\mathrm{SN}}$):} $G = -3.025$ (single saddle point).
    \item \textbf{Case 3 ($G > G_{\mathrm{SN}}$):} $G = 1.6$ (two equilibrium points).
\end{enumerate}

A trajectory was classified as \textbf{controlled} if it converged to a stable equilibrium point, and \textbf{uncontrolled} if it diverged toward $v \to -\infty$ (signaled by $v < -100$).

