\subsection*{Case 3: \(G=1.9>G_{\mathrm{SN}}\)}

For the final case, the quadratic formula gives two equilibria:
\[
(u_1^*,v_1^*)\approx(1.518,0.152),\qquad
(u_2^*,v_2^*)\approx(-12.518,-1.252).
\]
The Jacobian eigenvalues at the first point are approximately
\(-0.419\) and \(-3.351\), so it is a \textbf{stable node}.
At the second point, the eigenvalues are approximately
\(-0.119\) and \(11.789\), so it is a \textbf{saddle point}.

I tested six starting points to see whether the stable node
would attract all of them:
