\subsection*{Conclusions on Controllability}

Based on the simulations and the equilibrium calculations,
I can make the following statements for the parameters tested:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{For \(a=0.1\), \(b=1.0\), and \(G<-3.025\), the model
cannot settle at a finite equilibrium.} There are no real
steady states in this range, and all four trajectories I
simulated at \(G=-3.7\) were uncontrolled.

\item \textbf{For \(a=0.1\), \(b=1.0\), \(c=1.0\), and
\(G=-3.025\), none of my four tested initial conditions were
controlled.} The single equilibrium \((-5.5,-0.55)\) has an
unstable direction, although this does not rule out special
initial conditions that remain at or approach it.

\item \textbf{For \(a=0.1\), \(b=1.0\), \(c=1.0\), and \(G=1.9\),
the behaviour is controlled for the tested initial conditions
\((-6,-0.4)\), \((-7,-1.3)\), \((-4,-1.4)\), and \((-5,-0.6)\).}
These trajectories approach the stable node near
\((1.518,0.152)\). However, the tested points \((-8,-1.7)\)
and \((-9,-1.9)\) were uncontrolled.
\end{itemize}

These results suggest that \(G>G_{\mathrm{SN}}\) makes controlled
behaviour possible, but it does not guarantee control from
every starting point. The initial condition must also lie in
the region attracted to the stable equilibrium.

